\section{Initialization and Live Operation}
\label{sec:init_alignment}
\label{sec:startup-policy}

A private measurement-only Mahony observer runs during startup and continues
in Live to supply the levelling and sea-state front end. The main MEKF is not
used to produce its own startup signal or adaptation statistics. Startup
carries the observer, gravity-direction average, wave-period estimate,
band/statistics state and staged tuner through the Cold and TunerWarm stages.

\subsection{One-time handoff}
\label{sec:proxy-handoff}
The ordinary handoff checks elapsed warmup, sustained gravity alignment,
angular-rate and tuner-readiness conditions, together with heading availability.
The configured timeout is an alternative guarded path, not an unconditional
guarantee of entry at that time. On an actual handoff the wrapper initializes
the MEKF attitude and covariance, commits the current motion parameters and
integral regularizer, and starts Live with accelerometer-bias learning held.
The held and active configurations both retain the full 21-state covariance.
The source code and complete gate/entry equations are documented in the
\href{kalman_ou-w3d-stability-study.pdf}{companion stability study}.

\subsection{Magnetic acquisition and subsequent changes}
\label{sec:mag-two-stage}
A usable initial magnetic reference supplies heading; an ungauged handoff can
acquire heading later. Reference refinement, continuous hard-iron correction,
internal bias unlock and an external hold request have distinct effects. In
particular, attempted magnetic calls are counted separately from accepted
innovation updates, and an external hold can keep bias learning disabled.
Section~\ref{sec:mag-hi-apply} describes the continuous hard-iron path. A Live
tilt watchdog may also perform an accelerometer-based tilt reinitialization
while preserving yaw. The companion study treats all these events as explicit
hybrid maps rather than assuming an uninterrupted fixed-mode Kalman recursion.
